<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Herodium</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Herodium is a fortified palace built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-the-great">Herod the Great</a> on an artificially heightened, cone-shaped hill south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethlehem">Bethlehem</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bank">West Bank</a>. Herod chose it as his burial place, and in 2007 archaeologists led by Ehud Netzer identified what they believed to be his tomb on its slopes. Jewish rebels held it in the revolts against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> of 66–73 and 132–135.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herodium.jpg" alt="Herodium" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Asaf T. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herodium_from_above_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Herodium, in Greek Herodeion and in Arabic Jabal al-Fureidis, Mountain of the Little Paradise, is a fortified desert palace built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-the-great">Herod the Great</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a>, in the first century BCE. It stands atop a hill in the Judaean Desert about 12 kilometres south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and 5 kilometres southeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethlehem">Bethlehem</a>, at an elevation of 758 metres. The hill is artificial and cone-shaped, dominates the surrounding landscape and can be seen from Jerusalem. It is the only site named after Herod the Great. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> knew it as the Mountain of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, and Edward Robinson identified it as Herodium in 1841 from the description of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a>, Herod fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masada">Masada</a> in 40 BCE after the Parthian conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, clashed on the way with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> loyal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a> at this spot, and built a town there to commemorate his victory. At the summit his engineers built a fortified palace with double walls, towers, a Roman-style bathhouse, banquet halls and living quarters, and at the base a lower palace with gardens, courtyards and a large pool fed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aqueduct">aqueducts</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> says Herod was buried there after his death in 4 BCE. Jewish rebels held the site in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-jewish-roman-war">First Jewish–Roman War</a>, turning Herod's dining hall into a synagogue, until the Roman legate Sextus Lucilius Bassus captured it, likely in early 72 CE. Simon bar Kokhba made it a secondary headquarters in the revolt of 132–135 CE, after which it was abandoned.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Virgilio Canio Corbo and Stanislao Loffreda excavated the upper citadel from 1962 to 1967. From 1972 Ehud Netzer, for the Hebrew University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, worked mostly on the lower palace until his death in 2010. In 2007 he identified what he took to be the tomb of Herod on the northern slope, and in 2010 he found a royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> with frescoes of landscapes. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>-alloy ring found in 1968–1969 and cleaned in 2018 bears the Greek words of Pilates, and some have suggested it belonged to Pontius Pilate.</p><p>The site lies in Area C of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bank">West Bank</a>. Israel asserts that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo-accords">Oslo Accords</a> entitle it to work there, while Palestinian authorities say Israel has no right to dig or remove finds; the dispute was prominent in 2013, when finds from the site were shown at the Israel Museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Herodium” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He rebuilt the Temple on a vast platform, founded the harbour city of Caesarea Maritima and built palaces at Masada and <b>Herodium</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/herodian-kingdom-of-judea">Herodian Kingdom of Judea</a></li></ul></section>
